        <h2>Beijing Talks Produce Framework After 48 Hours of Negotiation</h2>
        <p>In what diplomatic sources are calling the most significant breakthrough since the Strait of Hormuz crisis began, Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi announced early Monday that a 60-day ceasefire framework has been agreed upon in principle by both the United States and Iran. The framework, reached after nearly 48 hours of continuous negotiations at the Diaoyutai State Guesthouse in Beijing, includes provisions for a phased reopening of the strait, limited sanctions relief, and the establishment of an international monitoring mechanism.</p>
        <p>The announcement came at a delicate moment — just hours before US military strikes against Iranian targets near Hormuz — and its durability now hangs in the balance. Nonetheless, the framework represents months of intensive Chinese diplomacy and the first time both parties have agreed to a written set of principles for de-escalation.</p>

        <h2>Key Provisions of the Framework</h2>
        <p>The framework consists of four main pillars. <strong>First</strong>, an immediate 60-day cessation of hostile military actions within 50 nautical miles of the Strait of Hormuz, covering both naval and aerial operations. <strong>Second</strong>, a phased reopening of the strait to commercial traffic, beginning with a "humanitarian corridor" for LNG and food-adjacent petroleum products within the first 14 days, followed by full commercial resumption conditional on compliance verification. <strong>Third</strong>, a limited sanctions relief package under which the US would unfreeze approximately $6 billion in Iranian oil revenue currently held in escrow accounts in South Korea and Iraq, disbursable only for humanitarian trade under UN oversight. <strong>Fourth</strong>, the creation of a Multilateral Strait Monitoring Commission (MSMC) with representatives from China, Oman, Qatar, and Switzerland, tasked with verifying compliance and investigating alleged violations.</p>

        <h2>Sticking Points and Unresolved Issues</h2>
        <p>Despite the framework's announcement, significant obstacles remain. The most contentious issue is <strong>sequencing</strong>: Iran insists that sanctions relief must precede any reopening of the strait, while the US maintains that a demonstrable resumption of shipping must come first. The current framework attempts to split the difference by using a 72-hour "confidence period" during which both actions would begin simultaneously, but neither side has formally committed to this timeline.</p>
        <p>Verification remains equally problematic. Iran has refused to accept any monitoring arrangement that involves US personnel or US-aligned states, while the US has rejected any framework that relies solely on Iranian self-reporting. The proposed MSMC represents a compromise, but its authority and access rights remain undefined. Additionally, the framework does not address Iran's demand for a permanent end to US naval presence in the Persian Gulf — a red line for Washington.</p>

