        <h2>International Reaction</h2>
        <p>The framework has received cautious endorsement from the international community. UN Secretary-General António Guterres called it "a necessary first step toward restoring the free flow of commerce through this critical waterway." The European Union's foreign policy chief voiced "measured optimism" while emphasizing that implementation would be the true test. Gulf Cooperation Council states, particularly Oman and Qatar, have offered to host the MSMC headquarters and provide logistical support for monitoring operations.</p>
        <p>However, the framework has its critics. Saudi Arabia has expressed private concerns that sanctions relief without ironclad verification could embolden Iran's regional activities beyond the strait. Israel has formally objected to any loosening of the sanctions regime, arguing that Iran's nuclear program remains unaddressed. Within the US Congress, reaction has split along predictable lines, with the administration's critics arguing that the framework concedes too much while supporters contend it is the only viable path to de-escalation.</p>

        <h2>Impact Assessment</h2>
        <p>The ceasefire framework represents a genuine diplomatic achievement, but its survival now depends on factors outside the negotiating room. The US military strikes on May 27 have introduced a wild card that could either concentrate minds on both sides or collapse the process entirely. If Iran chooses to treat the strikes as a violation of the framework before it is formally implemented, the entire diplomatic track could be set back by weeks or months.</p>
        <p>For the global economy, every day of delay costs approximately $2.4 billion in disrupted trade. The phased reopening timeline — even under optimal conditions — would not see full commercial traffic restored for at least 45-60 days. Energy markets are pricing in a prolonged disruption, with forward curves showing elevated premiums through Q3 2026. The framework offers hope, but hope alone does not move tankers through the strait.</p>
