        <h2>Brent Crude Breaks $102 Barrier</h2>
        <p>Global oil benchmarks surged on Monday as the collision of military escalation and fragile diplomacy created a perfect storm for energy markets. Brent crude settled at <strong>$102.30 per barrel</strong>, up $2.65 (2.7%) from the previous session's close of $99.65. The intraday range of $3.75 — with Brent touching $103.40 at the session peak — was the widest since March 2026, when the initial Hormuz closure sent prices spiraling from $78 to $94 in a single week.</p>
        <p>West Texas Intermediate (WTI) followed a similar trajectory, closing at $97.85, up $2.30 on the day. The Brent-WTI spread widened to $4.45, reflecting the particular vulnerability of waterborne crude shipments through the Strait of Hormuz that underpin Brent's physical delivery mechanism.</p>

        <h2>War Premium Expansion</h2>
        <p>The price surge is best understood as an expansion of what traders call the "Hormuz war premium" — the additional cost baked into oil prices to compensate for the risk of supply disruption. Before the crisis, Brent traded at $78.20 on February 27, the day before Iran declared the strait closed. By mid-March, the war premium had reached approximately $16 per barrel. Today, that premium stands at an estimated <strong>$24 per barrel</strong>, representing roughly 23% of the current price.</p>
        <p>What makes the current surge distinctive is its dual driver: both supply risk and demand uncertainty are moving in the same direction. The supply risk is obvious — military strikes near the strait increase the probability that Iran will retaliate against energy infrastructure or shipping. But demand uncertainty is also rising, as the global economic slowdown triggered by sustained high prices begins to feed back into consumption forecasts. The International Energy Agency revised its 2026 demand growth estimate downward by 400,000 barrels per day last week, citing "demand destruction in emerging markets."</p>

        <h2>Analyst Predictions</h2>
        <p>Wall Street analysts are divided on the near-term trajectory, but the consensus has shifted decisively toward higher prices. Goldman Sachs raised its 3-month Brent forecast to $108 per barrel, citing "an elevated floor under prices as long as military operations continue in the strait's vicinity." JPMorgan was more bearish, maintaining a $95 target but acknowledging "significant upside risk if the diplomatic track collapses entirely."</p>
