        <h2>Comparison to Previous Oil Shocks</h2>
        <p>The current price trajectory, while dramatic, still falls short of the most severe oil shocks in modern history. In inflation-adjusted terms, the 2008 peak of $147 per barrel (equivalent to approximately $210 in 2026 dollars) remains the benchmark. The 1973 Arab oil embargo quadrupled prices in percentage terms, though from a much lower base. The 2022 Russia-Ukraine shock saw Brent briefly exceed $127, but prices retreated within three months as alternative supplies were secured.</p>
        <p>What differentiates the Hormuz crisis is the physical constraint: roughly 18 million barrels per day of crude oil and 4.2 million barrels per day of LNG pass through the strait, representing approximately 20% of global oil consumption. No alternative route can compensate for this volume. The Cape of Good Hope rerouting can handle perhaps 60% of normal crude flows, but LNG cannot be redirected at all — there simply aren't enough LNG carriers to service European and Asian markets via the longer route.</p>

