        <h2>First Commercial Transit Since May 23</h2>
        <p>Three commercial vessels successfully transited the Strait of Hormuz on Monday morning, marking the first commercial passage through the waterway in 72 hours and providing a slender thread of hope that diplomatic channels may yet restore some flow of energy trade. The transit — comprising two LNG carriers and one crude oil tanker — was completed under an international naval escort arranged through the Omani-mediated diplomatic channel that also produced the ceasefire framework announced the same day.</p>
        <p>The vessels were identified as the <strong>Al Kharsaah</strong> (Qatar-flagged LNG carrier, 173,400 m³ capacity), the <strong>Grand Aniva</strong> (Marshall Islands-flagged LNG carrier, 155,000 m³ capacity), and the <strong>MT Atlantic Star</strong> (Liberian-flagged Suezmax crude oil tanker, 156,000 DWT). All three vessels had been anchored in the designated waiting area east of the strait for between four and six days, awaiting security clearance and escort availability.</p>

        <h2>Naval Escort Composition</h2>
        <p>The escort force represented an unprecedented multilateral arrangement. It comprised the French frigate <em>FS Provence</em>, the British destroyer <em>HMS Duncan</em>, and the Omani patrol vessel <em>Al Mabrukah</em>. Notably, no US Navy vessels participated in the escort — a deliberate decision aimed at reducing the risk of confrontation with IRGC naval forces, who have been most aggressive toward US-flagged and US-military assets.</p>
        <p>The transit began at 04:15 local time and was completed by 07:52, with the vessels maintaining a speed of 12 knots through the traffic separation scheme. Omani military helicopters provided overhead surveillance for the duration, and a US Navy P-8A Poseidon maintained radar coverage from outside the immediate strait area. IRGC patrol boats were observed at a distance of approximately 3 nautical miles but did not approach or interfere with the transit.</p>

        <h2>Insurance and Commercial Implications</h2>
        <p>The successful transit has significant implications for the maritime insurance market, which has been effectively frozen since all six major Protection and Indemnity (P&I) clubs withdrew Hormuz transit coverage in April. For these three vessels, specialized war risk insurance was arranged through Lloyd's of London at a premium of <strong>4.2% of hull value</strong> — roughly 20 times the pre-crisis rate. The total insurance cost for the three-vessel transit was estimated at $8.7 million, a figure that underscores the commercial unsustainability of continued disruptions.</p>

