        <h2>Significance and Future Prospects</h2>
        <p>While the successful transit is encouraging, its significance should not be overstated. Three vessels represent less than 3% of the strait's normal daily throughput of approximately 40-50 commercial ships. The transit was facilitated by extraordinary diplomatic and military coordination that cannot be sustained at scale. Moreover, the vessels involved were specifically selected for their non-US affiliation and their cargo of critical energy supplies — criteria that would exclude many of the tankers waiting on both sides of the strait.</p>
        <p>The insurance market remains the primary bottleneck. Even if security conditions improve, no shipowner can justify sending a $150 million vessel through the strait without war risk coverage, and current premiums make most voyages commercially unviable. The P&I clubs have indicated they will not restore coverage until there is a "sustained and verified" reduction in military threat levels — a condition that, by their own estimates, would require at least 30 consecutive days of incident-free transits.</p>

        <h2>Impact Assessment</h2>
