        <p>The US Navy's Operation Freedom of Navigation completed its fifth escorted transit through the Strait of Hormuz on Saturday, safely guiding a convoy of seven commercial vessels from the Persian Gulf to the Arabian Sea. The transit, which began at 02:30 local time and concluded at 06:15, represents the largest single convoy since the escort operation began on March 15, and the first to include LNG carriers among its complement.</p>
        <p>The convoy comprised three crude oil tankers (two VLCCs and one Suezmax), two product tankers carrying refined fuels, and two LNG carriers — the first LNG vessels to transit under US naval escort since the crisis began. All seven vessels were flagged to nations participating in the escort coalition: the United States, United Kingdom, France, Japan, and South Korea.</p>

        <h2>Naval Escort Composition</h2>
        <p>Convoy Five was escorted by a formidable naval force centered on the <strong>USS Eisenhower Carrier Strike Group</strong>, with the Ticonderoga-class cruiser USS Philippine Sea serving as the escort commander. Additional escort assets included the Arleigh Burke-class destroyer USS Farragut, the British Type 45 destroyer HMS Diamond, and the French frigate FS Auvergne. Air cover was provided by F/A-18E Super Hornets from VFA-143 and MH-60R Seahawk helicopters from HSM-48, conducting continuous anti-surface and anti-submarine surveillance.</p>
        <p>The escort formation employed a layered defense strategy: the destroyers maintained a 5-nautical-mile perimeter around the commercial vessels, with the Seahawk helicopters providing close-in surveillance and the carrier's combat air patrol maintaining radar coverage out to 80 nautical miles. Mine countermeasures vessels — the USS Gladiator and USS Scout — preceded the convoy by 3 hours, using sonar and unmanned underwater vehicles to sweep the designated shipping channels.</p>

        <h2>IRGC Speedboat Encounters</h2>
        <p>During the transit, the convoy encountered three separate groups of IRGC naval speedboats, totaling approximately 12 vessels. The first group of four Boghammar-class boats approached to within 800 meters of the convoy's eastern flank at approximately 03:45, retreating after the USS Farragut trained its Mark 45 gun mount in their direction and issued radio warnings on both international maritime channels and the IRGC's known tactical frequency.</p>
        <p>A second group of five speedboats attempted a more aggressive approach at 04:20, closing to approximately 500 meters and appearing to coordinate a pincer movement from the convoy's north and south. Two MH-60R Seahawks were dispatched to provide overwatch, and a P-8A Poseidon broadcast a recorded warning on emergency frequencies. The speedboats disengaged after approximately 12 minutes, but not before one vessel was observed filming the convoy with what analysts assessed to be military-grade surveillance equipment.</p>

