        <h2>Cost of Escort Operations</h2>
        <p>Operation Freedom of Navigation represents a significant and growing financial commitment. The US Department of Defense estimates that the operation costs approximately <strong>$14.2 million per day</strong> in direct operating costs — including fuel, ammunition, maintenance, and personnel — bringing the total expenditure since March 15 to roughly $1.04 billion. This figure does not include the opportunity cost of deploying the Eisenhower Carrier Strike Group to the region, nor the costs borne by coalition partners.</p>
        <p>The per-convoy cost is equally striking. When amortized across the seven vessels in Convoy Five, the escort cost amounts to approximately $2.03 million per vessel for a single transit — a figure that exceeds the typical freight revenue for many routes. For the LNG carriers, whose cargoes are worth $40-60 million at current prices, the escort cost is manageable; for product tankers carrying lower-value refined fuels, it represents a significant commercial burden.</p>

        <h2>Impact Assessment</h2>
        <p>The completion of Convoy Five demonstrates that the US-led escort operation can successfully protect commercial shipping through the strait, even in the face of active IRGC harassment. However, the operation's sustainability is increasingly in question. The Eisenhower Carrier Strike Group has been deployed for over 90 days, approaching the typical limit for high-tempo operations. Crew fatigue, equipment maintenance, and munitions replenishment will require a rotation within the next 30-45 days, and it remains unclear whether a replacement strike group can be made available without degrading commitments in the Indo-Pacific.</p>
        <p>More fundamentally, the escort operation addresses the symptom but not the cause. As long as Iran maintains the capability and will to threaten shipping — through mines, missiles, speedboats, or the mere threat of interdiction — the strait will remain commercially unviable for all but the most critical cargoes. The ceasefire framework announced on May 26 offers the only realistic path to a sustainable solution, but its prospects have been complicated by the US strikes on May 27. Until a comprehensive security arrangement is in place, each convoy will remain an expensive, risky, and fundamentally unsustainable exercise in crisis management.</p>
