        <p>Unlike crude oil, LNG cannot be piped over land, making this the most intractable aspect of the crisis. While oil can theoretically be redirected through pipelines, rail, and alternative maritime routes, natural gas in its liquefied form is entirely dependent on specialized vessels and receiving terminals — a supply chain that cannot be quickly reconfigured.</p>
        <p>Qatar, the world's largest LNG exporter, has declared force majeure on its Hormuz-dependent shipments, removing approximately 77 million tonnes per annum of LNG from the global market. This represents roughly 18% of global LNG trade and 24% of Europe's LNG imports. The impact is felt most acutely in Asia, where Japan, South Korea, and India depend on Qatari LNG for baseline power generation, and in Europe, where LNG imports had been growing as a substitute for Russian pipeline gas.</p>
